# hermes-agent-infra — environment reference for Coolify
#
# Do NOT put real values in this file and do NOT commit a populated .env.
# In Coolify, configure these under the resource's
# "Environment Variables" / "Secrets" tab instead. This file only documents
# what is required and what each variable does.

# --- Required: dashboard public URL -------------------------------------

# Public HTTPS URL Coolify assigns to this service, e.g. https://hermes.example.com
# Required so the dashboard's auth gate builds correct session/OAuth-callback
# URLs behind Coolify's reverse proxy. Must match the domain configured in
# Coolify exactly, scheme included.
HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL=https://hermes.example.com

# --- Required: dashboard basic auth --------------------------------------
#
# The dashboard always requires an auth provider when bound to a
# non-loopback address (confirmed in Hermes Agent's own docs — there is no
# "expose without auth" option). Basic auth is enough for a personal,
# single-user deployment behind Coolify's HTTPS.

# Login username for the dashboard.
HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_USERNAME=replace-with-a-username

# Scrypt hash of the login password — never store the plaintext password.
# Generate with (replace TU_PASSWORD, then discard the plaintext). This
# command was verified against the real image: it boots the full gateway
# (syncs 82 bundled skills first, ~10-15s of noisy s6 boot logs) before
# printing the hash on its own line just before shutdown — that's expected,
# not a hang. Requires Docker running; on Windows use Git Bash for the
# backslash line continuation (PowerShell needs a backtick ` instead):
#   docker run --rm nousresearch/hermes-agent:v2026.8.27 \
#     python -c "from plugins.dashboard_auth.basic import hash_password; print(hash_password('TU_PASSWORD'))"
#
# IMPORTANT — escape every literal $ as $$ when pasting into Coolify's
# Environment Variables UI. Confirmed empirically: Coolify applies
# Compose-style $VAR interpolation to stored values before injecting them
# into the container. The hash's scrypt$N$r$p$salt$digest format contains
# several $-delimited segments — any segment starting with a LETTER right
# after a $ (e.g. the salt, which is base64 and often starts with a letter)
# gets silently swallowed as an "undefined variable" and replaced with an
# empty string, corrupting the hash without any visible error. Segments
# starting with a digit (like the N/r/p cost parameters) survive untouched,
# since no variable name can start with a digit — that's why only PART of
# the hash goes missing, not all of it, which makes this easy to miss.
# Verify after pasting by checking the resource's Terminal tab:
#   echo "$HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH"
# It must print back with single $ characters, matching the hash you
# generated — if any $-prefixed segment is missing, you forgot to escape.
HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH=replace-with-generated-hash-dollars-escaped-as-double-dollar-for-coolify

# Signs the dashboard's session cookies.
# Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-secret

# --- Required: gateway API key (shared by hermes-webui) ------------------
#
# Enables the agent's gateway API server (port 8642, internal-only — never
# exposed to Coolify/the internet). hermes-webui uses this to reach the
# gateway for its Tasks/cron ticking and "gateway reachable" status pill.
# Must be at least 16 characters; without it the agent still runs
# messaging + cron fine, but port 8642 stays unbound and webui reports
# "Gateway endpoint not reachable" (non-fatal, just loses those features).
# Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 24
API_SERVER_KEY=replace-with-a-random-key-at-least-16-chars

# --- Required: hermes-webui login password --------------------------------
#
# hermes-webui has its OWN password auth, separate from the built-in
# dashboard's basic auth above — it's a single shared password (no
# username field). Required since the public domain routes here.
# Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 18
HERMES_WEBUI_PASSWORD=replace-with-a-strong-password

# --- Notes on variables NOT in this file --------------------------------
#
# HERMES_DASHBOARD, HERMES_UID and HERMES_GID are fixed in
# docker-compose.yml for this deployment and do not need to be set here.
#
# No LLM provider API keys (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, etc.) are
# configured via environment variables in this deployment. Configuring the
# model provider (OmniRoute) is NOT possible from either web UI — confirmed
# empirically against a real deployment: neither the built-in dashboard's
# "Custom endpoint" picker nor hermes-webui's Settings → Providers list
# expose base_url/api_key fields for a custom OpenAI-compatible endpoint
# (this is a known, currently open upstream gap — see
# NousResearch/hermes-agent issue #42042). It has to be done by editing
# `config.yaml` directly. See README "Conectar OmniRoute como provider"
# for the exact commands.

# --- Off-site backups (scripts/backup.sh, run on the host via cron) -----
#
# These are NOT read by the docker-compose.yml services — they configure
# scripts/backup.sh directly, which is invoked from a host crontab, not
# through Coolify. See README "Backups" for full setup instructions.
#
# Reuses the same Backblaze B2 bucket/repository as ai-gateway-infra's
# OmniRoute backups — restic distinguishes snapshots by --host and --tag,
# so a separate bucket isn't needed.

# restic repository target — the same bucket already used for OmniRoute:
#   b2:your-bucket-name:omniroute-backups
RESTIC_REPOSITORY=b2:your-bucket-name:omniroute-backups

# Encrypts the restic repository. A restic repository has exactly ONE
# password shared by everything backed up into it — if reusing OmniRoute's
# bucket, reuse the SAME password already generated for that repository.
# Generate a NEW one only if this is a fresh, separate repository:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
RESTIC_PASSWORD=replace-with-the-repository-password

# Backblaze B2 application key (same one used for OmniRoute's backups if
# reusing the bucket — needs read AND write/delete, see ai-gateway-infra
# README "Backups" for why).
B2_ACCOUNT_ID=replace-with-b2-key-id
B2_ACCOUNT_KEY=replace-with-b2-application-key
